1
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:13,500
More than three hundred thousand kilometres

2
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:14,060
from Earth,

3
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:16,436
an explosion shakes a spacecraft

4
00:00:16,586 --> 00:00:18,416
Oxygen is pouring out into space

5
00:00:18,416 --> 00:00:19,496
The power is dying

6
00:00:19,496 --> 00:00:21,612
The gauges are falling toward zero

7
00:00:21,762 --> 00:00:23,912
Three astronauts are forced to hide in a tiny

8
00:00:23,912 --> 00:00:25,512
cabin built for just two people,

9
00:00:25,512 --> 00:00:27,055
for about two days

10
00:00:27,205 --> 00:00:29,304
And they are almost four days from home

11
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:30,764
This is Apollo 13,

12
00:00:30,764 --> 00:00:32,924
the Moon mission that became the most famous

13
00:00:32,924 --> 00:00:35,004
survival story in history

14
00:00:35,211 --> 00:00:36,801
How do you fit a square box into a

15
00:00:36,801 --> 00:00:37,441
round hole?

16
00:00:37,441 --> 00:00:39,881
Why did a few minutes of silence feel endless?

17
00:00:39,881 --> 00:00:42,197
And who brought them home?

18
00:00:44,783 --> 00:00:45,973
April 1970,

19
00:00:45,973 --> 00:00:48,253
less than a year after humans first walked on

20
00:00:48,253 --> 00:00:49,370
the Moon

21
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,990
Apollo 13 was the third mission sent to land

22
00:00:51,990 --> 00:00:52,590
on the Moon,

23
00:00:52,590 --> 00:00:54,990
in the Fra Mauro highlands

24
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:56,370
The commander was Jim Lovell,

25
00:00:56,370 --> 00:00:58,690
a veteran who had already flown around the Moon

26
00:00:58,690 --> 00:01:00,285
on Apollo 8

27
00:01:00,435 --> 00:01:01,425
With him were Fred Haise,

28
00:01:01,425 --> 00:01:02,785
the lunar module pilot,

29
00:01:02,785 --> 00:01:03,785
and Ken Mattingly,

30
00:01:03,785 --> 00:01:05,865
the command module pilot

31
00:01:06,055 --> 00:01:07,205
But a week before launch,

32
00:01:07,205 --> 00:01:09,605
a backup astronaut caught German measles,

33
00:01:09,605 --> 00:01:12,035
and the whole crew was exposed

34
00:01:12,185 --> 00:01:14,575
Tests showed only Mattingly had no immunity

35
00:01:14,575 --> 00:01:15,775
Two days before launch,

36
00:01:15,775 --> 00:01:17,690
he was pulled from the flight

37
00:01:17,840 --> 00:01:20,390
His replacement was backup pilot Jack Swigert,

38
00:01:20,390 --> 00:01:22,990
with only days to train with his new crew

39
00:01:23,367 --> 00:01:24,757
Mattingly never caught measles

40
00:01:24,757 --> 00:01:26,517
But being left on the ground would later turn

41
00:01:26,517 --> 00:01:29,117
out to be a stroke of luck

42
00:01:32,017 --> 00:01:33,167
On April the eleventh,

43
00:01:33,167 --> 00:01:33,927
1970,

44
00:01:33,927 --> 00:01:36,607
at exactly 13:13 Houston time,

45
00:01:36,607 --> 00:01:39,207
the Saturn V rocket left the launch pad

46
00:01:39,727 --> 00:01:41,037
Just minutes into the climb,

47
00:01:41,037 --> 00:01:43,477
one engine on the second stage shut down early

48
00:01:43,477 --> 00:01:45,197
The others had to burn longer to make up

49
00:01:45,197 --> 00:01:46,183
for it

50
00:01:46,333 --> 00:01:47,283
That was the first problem

51
00:01:47,283 --> 00:01:49,123
But the spacecraft still reached the right

52
00:01:49,123 --> 00:01:49,563
orbit,

53
00:01:49,563 --> 00:01:51,292
and headed for the Moon

54
00:01:51,442 --> 00:01:52,552
The ship had two parts:

55
00:01:52,552 --> 00:01:54,032
the command module Odyssey,

56
00:01:54,032 --> 00:01:55,752
where all three men lived and worked,

57
00:01:55,752 --> 00:01:57,472
and the lunar module Aquarius,

58
00:01:57,472 --> 00:01:59,392
which would take two of them down to the

59
00:01:59,392 --> 00:02:00,360
Moon

60
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:02,780
The first two days were so smooth that Mission

61
00:02:02,780 --> 00:02:04,820
Control in Houston joked they were bored to

62
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:05,887
tears

63
00:02:06,037 --> 00:02:07,947
On the evening of April the thirteenth,

64
00:02:07,947 --> 00:02:10,267
the crew broadcast live TV from space

65
00:02:10,267 --> 00:02:11,787
But the big networks didn't air it

66
00:02:11,787 --> 00:02:14,235
Going to the Moon had become routine

67
00:02:14,385 --> 00:02:16,075
A few minutes after the broadcast,

68
00:02:16,075 --> 00:02:18,615
Houston asked Swigert to switch on the fans

69
00:02:18,615 --> 00:02:20,335
that stirred the oxygen tanks,

70
00:02:20,335 --> 00:02:22,026
a routine chore

71
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:23,646
Swigert flipped the switch

72
00:02:23,646 --> 00:02:24,926
Just over a minute later,

73
00:02:24,926 --> 00:02:27,006
the whole spacecraft shook

74
00:02:30,417 --> 00:02:31,167
A loud bang

75
00:02:31,167 --> 00:02:32,887
Warning lights flared across the control

76
00:02:32,887 --> 00:02:33,944
panels

77
00:02:34,094 --> 00:02:35,164
Swigert radioed:

78
00:02:35,164 --> 00:02:35,484
Houston,

79
00:02:35,484 --> 00:02:36,764
we've had a problem here

80
00:02:36,764 --> 00:02:37,944
Lovell repeated it:

81
00:02:37,944 --> 00:02:38,324
Houston,

82
00:02:38,324 --> 00:02:39,990
we've had a problem

83
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:40,850
At that moment,

84
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:42,930
the ship was about three hundred and thirty

85
00:02:42,930 --> 00:02:44,946
thousand kilometres from Earth

86
00:02:45,096 --> 00:02:45,726
In Houston,

87
00:02:45,726 --> 00:02:48,046
the screens showed numbers that made no sense

88
00:02:48,046 --> 00:02:48,606
At first,

89
00:02:48,606 --> 00:02:51,206
many thought it was just a faulty sensor

90
00:02:51,363 --> 00:02:52,833
Then Lovell looked out of the window,

91
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:55,073
and saw a white cloud of gas venting into

92
00:02:55,073 --> 00:02:56,097
space

93
00:02:56,247 --> 00:02:57,037
It was oxygen

94
00:02:57,037 --> 00:02:58,837
One oxygen tank had exploded,

95
00:02:58,837 --> 00:03:00,645
and the other was leaking

96
00:03:00,795 --> 00:03:02,225
Oxygen wasn't just for breathing

97
00:03:02,225 --> 00:03:04,465
It also fed the fuel cells that made Odyssey's

98
00:03:04,465 --> 00:03:06,225
electricity and water

99
00:03:06,528 --> 00:03:08,638
Flight director Gene Kranz told the room:

100
00:03:08,638 --> 00:03:09,838
let's solve the problem,

101
00:03:09,838 --> 00:03:12,438
but let's not make it any worse by guessing

102
00:03:12,701 --> 00:03:13,931
But the clock was already ticking

103
00:03:13,931 --> 00:03:16,531
Odyssey was running out of power

104
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:21,620
Less than two hours after the explosion,

105
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:24,140
Odyssey had only about fifteen minutes of power

106
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:25,046
left

107
00:03:25,196 --> 00:03:27,386
All three had to climb into the lunar module

108
00:03:27,386 --> 00:03:27,986
Aquarius,

109
00:03:27,986 --> 00:03:30,236
and turn it into a lifeboat

110
00:03:30,386 --> 00:03:32,616
Swigert shut down Odyssey system by system

111
00:03:32,616 --> 00:03:34,356
Its batteries had to be saved for the trip

112
00:03:34,356 --> 00:03:36,215
back through the atmosphere

113
00:03:36,365 --> 00:03:38,235
But Aquarius was built to keep two people

114
00:03:38,235 --> 00:03:38,715
alive,

115
00:03:38,715 --> 00:03:40,477
for about two days

116
00:03:40,627 --> 00:03:42,497
Now it had to keep three people alive,

117
00:03:42,497 --> 00:03:44,413
for almost four days

118
00:03:44,563 --> 00:03:45,633
And there was a bigger problem:

119
00:03:45,633 --> 00:03:47,233
they were heading for the Moon on a path

120
00:03:47,233 --> 00:03:49,045
that would not swing back to Earth on its

121
00:03:49,045 --> 00:03:49,987
own

122
00:03:50,137 --> 00:03:51,887
Five hours after the explosion,

123
00:03:51,887 --> 00:03:54,327
Aquarius fired its engine for just over thirty

124
00:03:54,327 --> 00:03:54,887
seconds,

125
00:03:54,887 --> 00:03:56,927
putting them on a course around the Moon and

126
00:03:56,927 --> 00:03:57,882
back

127
00:03:58,032 --> 00:03:58,662
They had a way home

128
00:03:58,662 --> 00:04:00,662
But that road ran around the far side of

129
00:04:00,662 --> 00:04:01,142
the Moon,

130
00:04:01,142 --> 00:04:03,742
where no one could reach them

131
00:04:06,617 --> 00:04:08,507
On the evening of April the fourteenth,

132
00:04:08,507 --> 00:04:09,447
Houston time,

133
00:04:09,447 --> 00:04:11,487
Apollo 13 passed behind the Moon

134
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:13,584
Contact with Earth was cut

135
00:04:13,734 --> 00:04:15,764
They flew past about two hundred and fifty

136
00:04:15,764 --> 00:04:17,718
kilometres above the surface

137
00:04:17,868 --> 00:04:19,658
Because their path swung wider than other

138
00:04:19,658 --> 00:04:20,218
missions,

139
00:04:20,218 --> 00:04:22,458
they became the humans farthest from Earth in

140
00:04:22,458 --> 00:04:23,018
history:

141
00:04:23,018 --> 00:04:25,618
more than four hundred thousand kilometres

142
00:04:25,949 --> 00:04:27,779
That record stood for fifty-six years,

143
00:04:27,779 --> 00:04:30,379
until the Artemis II mission passed it in April

144
00:04:30,379 --> 00:04:31,499
2026

145
00:04:32,312 --> 00:04:34,782
Swigert and Haise took photo after photo

146
00:04:34,782 --> 00:04:37,142
Lovell quietly looked down at a land he would

147
00:04:37,142 --> 00:04:38,722
never walk on

148
00:04:38,872 --> 00:04:40,422
About twenty-five minutes later,

149
00:04:40,422 --> 00:04:42,984
the ship reappeared on the other side

150
00:04:43,134 --> 00:04:44,684
But the numbers in Houston were grim:

151
00:04:44,684 --> 00:04:45,804
at their current speed,

152
00:04:45,804 --> 00:04:47,884
they would run out of supplies before they got

153
00:04:47,884 --> 00:04:49,004
home

154
00:04:52,117 --> 00:04:53,707
Two hours after rounding the Moon,

155
00:04:53,707 --> 00:04:55,827
Aquarius fired its engine once more,

156
00:04:55,827 --> 00:04:57,645
for over four minutes

157
00:04:57,795 --> 00:04:59,745
That burn brought them home about ten hours

158
00:04:59,745 --> 00:05:00,780
sooner

159
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:01,880
To save power,

160
00:05:01,880 --> 00:05:03,800
almost everything was switched off:

161
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:04,520
the heaters,

162
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:05,440
the computers,

163
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:07,131
most of the lights

164
00:05:07,281 --> 00:05:09,551
Aquarius ran on about a fifth of its normal

165
00:05:09,551 --> 00:05:10,557
power

166
00:05:10,707 --> 00:05:12,657
Water was even more precious than power,

167
00:05:12,657 --> 00:05:14,257
because it cooled the equipment

168
00:05:14,257 --> 00:05:16,557
Each man drank only about a hundred and eighty

169
00:05:16,557 --> 00:05:17,999
millilitres a day

170
00:05:18,149 --> 00:05:18,739
In Houston,

171
00:05:18,739 --> 00:05:21,099
hundreds of engineers worked day and night,

172
00:05:21,099 --> 00:05:23,259
counting every bit of power and every drop of

173
00:05:23,259 --> 00:05:24,269
water

174
00:05:24,419 --> 00:05:25,729
Then a new number appeared,

175
00:05:25,729 --> 00:05:27,329
scarier than all the rest:

176
00:05:27,329 --> 00:05:29,929
the carbon dioxide in the cabin was rising

177
00:05:34,050 --> 00:05:36,440
Every breath we take releases carbon dioxide

178
00:05:36,440 --> 00:05:37,400
In a sealed cabin,

179
00:05:37,400 --> 00:05:38,640
if it isn't filtered out,

180
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:40,994
it poisons the people breathing it

181
00:05:41,144 --> 00:05:43,214
Aquarius had canisters filled with a chemical

182
00:05:43,214 --> 00:05:45,614
that soaked up carbon dioxide

183
00:05:45,777 --> 00:05:47,727
But those canisters were only meant for two

184
00:05:47,727 --> 00:05:48,127
people,

185
00:05:48,127 --> 00:05:50,272
for about a day and a half

186
00:05:50,422 --> 00:05:52,372
Odyssey had plenty of spare canisters

187
00:05:52,372 --> 00:05:54,173
But there was a problem

188
00:05:54,323 --> 00:05:55,873
Odyssey's canisters were square

189
00:05:55,873 --> 00:05:58,353
The sockets in Aquarius were round

190
00:05:58,503 --> 00:06:00,453
The two modules were built by two different

191
00:06:00,453 --> 00:06:00,933
companies,

192
00:06:00,933 --> 00:06:03,533
and nobody expected them to ever share parts

193
00:06:04,413 --> 00:06:06,123
The carbon dioxide kept rising

194
00:06:06,123 --> 00:06:06,883
Without a fix,

195
00:06:06,883 --> 00:06:09,483
the air in the cabin would become dangerous

196
00:06:09,743 --> 00:06:10,253
In Houston,

197
00:06:10,253 --> 00:06:12,333
a team of engineers dumped everything the crew

198
00:06:12,333 --> 00:06:13,773
had on board onto a table,

199
00:06:13,773 --> 00:06:14,293
and asked:

200
00:06:14,293 --> 00:06:16,253
how do you fit a square box into a

201
00:06:16,253 --> 00:06:17,693
round hole?

202
00:06:21,050 --> 00:06:23,240
The engineers could only use exactly what the

203
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:24,848
crew had on board

204
00:06:24,998 --> 00:06:25,828
Plastic bags,

205
00:06:25,828 --> 00:06:27,988
cardboard covers from the flight manuals,

206
00:06:27,988 --> 00:06:29,188
hoses from the spacesuits,

207
00:06:29,188 --> 00:06:30,028
a sock,

208
00:06:30,028 --> 00:06:31,593
and duct tape

209
00:06:31,743 --> 00:06:33,533
They built a test version on the ground,

210
00:06:33,533 --> 00:06:35,493
then read the steps up to the crew by

211
00:06:35,493 --> 00:06:36,613
radio

212
00:06:36,829 --> 00:06:37,339
On board,

213
00:06:37,339 --> 00:06:39,979
Swigert and Haise followed every instruction,

214
00:06:39,979 --> 00:06:42,579
like building a model kit in space

215
00:06:42,855 --> 00:06:44,905
The strange contraption was nicknamed the

216
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:45,325
mailbox,

217
00:06:45,325 --> 00:06:47,222
because it looked like one

218
00:06:47,372 --> 00:06:48,922
Then came the moment of truth:

219
00:06:48,922 --> 00:06:50,002
switch on the fan,

220
00:06:50,002 --> 00:06:52,602
and watch the carbon dioxide gauge

221
00:06:52,910 --> 00:06:54,020
The needle started to fall

222
00:06:54,020 --> 00:06:54,660
In Houston,

223
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:56,708
the whole room breathed again

224
00:06:56,858 --> 00:06:57,568
The air was saved

225
00:06:57,568 --> 00:07:00,008
But another enemy was creeping into the cabin:

226
00:07:00,008 --> 00:07:01,448
the cold

227
00:07:04,683 --> 00:07:05,513
The heaters were off

228
00:07:05,513 --> 00:07:06,473
Inside Odyssey,

229
00:07:06,473 --> 00:07:08,807
the temperature dropped to about three degrees

230
00:07:08,807 --> 00:07:09,927
Celsius

231
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:12,531
Water droplets formed on the walls and panels

232
00:07:12,531 --> 00:07:14,331
No one could sleep for more than a few

233
00:07:14,331 --> 00:07:15,355
hours

234
00:07:15,505 --> 00:07:16,335
Water was scarce,

235
00:07:16,335 --> 00:07:17,555
and all three grew gaunt

236
00:07:17,555 --> 00:07:20,155
Lovell alone lost more than six kilos

237
00:07:20,660 --> 00:07:22,290
Fred Haise came down with a fever

238
00:07:22,290 --> 00:07:24,450
Drinking so little had given him a urinary

239
00:07:24,450 --> 00:07:25,570
infection

240
00:07:25,873 --> 00:07:27,943
Then Houston saw that the ship was slowly

241
00:07:27,943 --> 00:07:29,183
drifting off its course home

242
00:07:29,183 --> 00:07:31,610
They needed one more engine burn

243
00:07:31,760 --> 00:07:33,830
But the guidance computer had been shut down to

244
00:07:33,830 --> 00:07:34,430
save power

245
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:34,950
This time,

246
00:07:34,950 --> 00:07:37,474
they had to fly entirely by hand

247
00:07:37,624 --> 00:07:39,694
They used the line between day and night on

248
00:07:39,694 --> 00:07:41,534
Earth as a sighting mark in the window,

249
00:07:41,534 --> 00:07:43,314
holding the ship steady through a

250
00:07:43,314 --> 00:07:44,754
fourteen-second burn

251
00:07:45,008 --> 00:07:45,638
The burn worked

252
00:07:45,638 --> 00:07:47,358
But one huge question remained:

253
00:07:47,358 --> 00:07:49,958
could Odyssey be brought back to life?

254
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:55,100
Odyssey had been switched off for days,

255
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:57,700
freezing cold and soaked with condensation

256
00:07:58,169 --> 00:08:00,199
No one had ever restarted a command module in

257
00:08:00,199 --> 00:08:02,559
space under conditions like that

258
00:08:02,709 --> 00:08:03,219
Normally,

259
00:08:03,219 --> 00:08:05,019
writing a procedure like that took three

260
00:08:05,019 --> 00:08:05,379
months

261
00:08:05,379 --> 00:08:07,401
Houston had three days

262
00:08:07,551 --> 00:08:08,381
Ken Mattingly,

263
00:08:08,381 --> 00:08:10,141
the man grounded over measles,

264
00:08:10,141 --> 00:08:12,421
worked with the engineers to test every step

265
00:08:12,421 --> 00:08:14,890
again and again in the simulator

266
00:08:15,040 --> 00:08:16,630
Every switch they turned on used power

267
00:08:16,630 --> 00:08:18,550
They had to find exactly the right order,

268
00:08:18,550 --> 00:08:20,270
without going over what was left in the

269
00:08:20,270 --> 00:08:21,390
batteries

270
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:22,510
There was one more fear:

271
00:08:22,510 --> 00:08:24,750
the condensation could cause short circuits

272
00:08:24,750 --> 00:08:26,837
when the power came back on

273
00:08:26,987 --> 00:08:27,377
At last,

274
00:08:27,377 --> 00:08:29,177
the procedure was read up to the ship

275
00:08:29,177 --> 00:08:31,817
Swigert filled every blank scrap of paper with

276
00:08:31,817 --> 00:08:32,898
notes

277
00:08:33,048 --> 00:08:34,278
On April the seventeenth,

278
00:08:34,278 --> 00:08:35,298
1970,

279
00:08:35,298 --> 00:08:37,478
Odyssey's systems lit up one by one

280
00:08:37,478 --> 00:08:38,158
No sparks

281
00:08:38,158 --> 00:08:39,830
The ship was alive

282
00:08:39,980 --> 00:08:41,090
But before going home,

283
00:08:41,090 --> 00:08:42,330
they had to say goodbye,

284
00:08:42,330 --> 00:08:44,650
and see for the first time what the explosion

285
00:08:44,650 --> 00:08:46,090
had done

286
00:08:49,217 --> 00:08:50,927
About four hours before landing,

287
00:08:50,927 --> 00:08:52,567
they released the service module,

288
00:08:52,567 --> 00:08:55,167
the part that held the oxygen tanks

289
00:08:55,417 --> 00:08:56,947
As it drifted past the window,

290
00:08:56,947 --> 00:08:58,047
Lovell was stunned:

291
00:08:58,047 --> 00:09:00,647
one whole side of the spacecraft was missing

292
00:09:01,211 --> 00:09:01,881
A new fear:

293
00:09:01,881 --> 00:09:03,961
had the explosion damaged the heat shield?

294
00:09:03,961 --> 00:09:04,481
If so,

295
00:09:04,481 --> 00:09:06,121
they would burn up on the way through the

296
00:09:06,121 --> 00:09:07,241
atmosphere

297
00:09:07,574 --> 00:09:09,524
The investigation later found the cause

298
00:09:09,524 --> 00:09:11,644
Oxygen tank number two had been dropped in

299
00:09:11,644 --> 00:09:12,764
1968

300
00:09:13,693 --> 00:09:14,203
Inside it,

301
00:09:14,203 --> 00:09:16,323
the safety switches were only rated for

302
00:09:16,323 --> 00:09:17,123
twenty-eight volts,

303
00:09:17,123 --> 00:09:19,218
while the system had been upgraded to

304
00:09:19,218 --> 00:09:20,324
sixty-five

305
00:09:20,474 --> 00:09:21,504
During a ground test,

306
00:09:21,504 --> 00:09:23,424
the tank's heaters were left on for eight

307
00:09:23,424 --> 00:09:23,864
hours,

308
00:09:23,864 --> 00:09:26,464
burning the insulation off the wires

309
00:09:26,674 --> 00:09:26,944
Then,

310
00:09:26,944 --> 00:09:27,584
in space,

311
00:09:27,584 --> 00:09:29,424
when the stirring fan was switched on,

312
00:09:29,424 --> 00:09:31,877
a spark set off the explosion

313
00:09:32,027 --> 00:09:33,377
About an hour before landing,

314
00:09:33,377 --> 00:09:34,897
they left Aquarius behind

315
00:09:34,897 --> 00:09:36,177
Houston said goodbye:

316
00:09:36,177 --> 00:09:36,877
Farewell,

317
00:09:36,877 --> 00:09:37,577
Aquarius,

318
00:09:37,577 --> 00:09:39,168
and we thank you

319
00:09:39,318 --> 00:09:41,188
Now only one last test remained:

320
00:09:41,188 --> 00:09:43,428
plunging into the atmosphere at nearly forty

321
00:09:43,428 --> 00:09:45,508
thousand kilometres an hour

322
00:09:48,883 --> 00:09:49,753
During reentry,

323
00:09:49,753 --> 00:09:52,313
the fiery plasma around the capsule blocks all

324
00:09:52,313 --> 00:09:53,233
radio waves

325
00:09:53,233 --> 00:09:53,793
Normally,

326
00:09:53,793 --> 00:09:56,393
that silence lasts about three minutes

327
00:09:56,755 --> 00:09:57,785
Three minutes passed

328
00:09:57,785 --> 00:09:58,545
Houston called

329
00:09:58,545 --> 00:09:59,903
No answer

330
00:10:00,053 --> 00:10:00,763
Four minutes

331
00:10:00,763 --> 00:10:01,683
Still silence

332
00:10:01,683 --> 00:10:03,709
Millions held their breath in front of their

333
00:10:03,709 --> 00:10:04,745
TVs

334
00:10:04,895 --> 00:10:07,205
The silence lasted nearly a minute and a half

335
00:10:07,205 --> 00:10:08,285
longer than expected,

336
00:10:08,285 --> 00:10:10,701
longer than on any flight before

337
00:10:10,851 --> 00:10:12,381
Then a voice came over the speakers:

338
00:10:12,381 --> 00:10:12,921
Okay,

339
00:10:12,921 --> 00:10:13,361
Joe

340
00:10:13,361 --> 00:10:14,963
It was Swigert

341
00:10:15,113 --> 00:10:17,223
Three red and white parachutes blossomed over

342
00:10:17,223 --> 00:10:19,108
the South Pacific sky

343
00:10:19,258 --> 00:10:20,648
On April the seventeenth,

344
00:10:20,648 --> 00:10:21,688
1970,

345
00:10:21,688 --> 00:10:23,088
Odyssey splashed down,

346
00:10:23,088 --> 00:10:25,488
only about six kilometres from the recovery

347
00:10:25,488 --> 00:10:26,400
ship

348
00:10:26,550 --> 00:10:27,060
In Houston,

349
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:28,420
the control room erupted

350
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:29,540
People lit cigars,

351
00:10:29,540 --> 00:10:31,230
clapped and hugged

352
00:10:31,380 --> 00:10:33,530
The company that built Aquarius even sent a

353
00:10:33,530 --> 00:10:35,650
joke invoice for over four hundred thousand

354
00:10:35,650 --> 00:10:37,890
dollars to the company that built Odyssey:

355
00:10:37,890 --> 00:10:40,490
a towing fee for the trip home

356
00:10:40,917 --> 00:10:42,987
Apollo 13 never landed on the Moon

357
00:10:42,987 --> 00:10:45,389
It has been called a successful failure

358
00:10:45,539 --> 00:10:47,729
Jim Lovell never got to walk on the Moon

359
00:10:47,729 --> 00:10:50,129
Ken Mattingly flew to the Moon two years later,

360
00:10:50,129 --> 00:10:51,834
on Apollo 16

361
00:10:51,984 --> 00:10:53,414
If you were inside Aquarius,

362
00:10:53,414 --> 00:10:54,734
what would scare you most:

363
00:10:54,734 --> 00:10:55,294
the cold,

364
00:10:55,294 --> 00:10:55,934
the thirst,

365
00:10:55,934 --> 00:10:56,894
or the poison air?

366
00:10:56,894 --> 00:10:58,441
Tell me in the comments

367
00:10:58,591 --> 00:11:01,191
Subscribe so you don't miss the next episode
